\subsection{Elaborating a Runtime Index into a Bounded Witness}

An untrusted integer from a user interface is not already a
valid index into a vector. Put the check at the boundary by
constructing a witness of an indexed type. Use $\mathrm{Fin}(n)$
with constructors $\mathrm{fzero}:\mathrm{Fin}(n+1)$ and
$\mathrm{fsuc}:\mathrm{Fin}(n)\to\mathrm{Fin}(n+1)$. The
procedure accepts a natural size $n$ and a mathematical integer
$i$; it returns either failure or a term of $\mathrm{Fin}(n)$
representing exactly $i$. An option wrapper distinguishes failure
from $\mathrm{fzero}$, which represents a valid index zero.
The recursive call decreases the size, exposing the index required
by the successor constructor.

\begin{sudocode}[Build a witness for a checked index]
\Function{Bounded}{$n,i$}
  \If{$n=0$ or $i<0$}
    \State \Return none \Comment{No index of an empty vector}
  \EndIf
  \If{$i=0$}
    \State \Return $\mathrm{some}(\mathrm{fzero})$
  \EndIf
  \State $r\gets\Call{Bounded}{n-1,i-1}$
  \If{$r=\mathrm{none}$}
    \State \Return none
  \EndIf
  \State Read $r$ as $\mathrm{some}(j)$
  \State \Return $\mathrm{some}(\mathrm{fsuc}(j))$
  \Comment{Lift the smaller witness}
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
With a three-element vector $[8,5,2]$, call
\textsc{Bounded}$(3,2)$. The next calls are $(2,1)$ and $(1,0)$.
The latter constructs $\mathrm{fzero}:\mathrm{Fin}(1)$.
Returning twice wraps it in two successors, yielding a term of
$\mathrm{Fin}(3)$. A vector lookup follows the first successor
past $8$, the second past $5$, and stops at $2$ when it reads
$\mathrm{fzero}$. For $(3,3)$, the calls instead end at
$(0,0)$; checking size before recognizing index zero is essential
here, and failure propagates to the caller.

\Cref{fig:visual-10} contrasts the successful construction with the
one-past-the-end call.

