\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
Decode $\mathrm{fzero}$ as zero and a successor as one plus the
decoded predecessor. Induction on $n$ gives success exactly when
$0\le i<n$, with a witness decoding to $i$; testing the two
traces alone does not establish that claim. This unary construction
uses at most $n+1$ calls. Implementations with large indices may
prefer a natural number paired with a proof of a bound, to avoid
allocating a chain of successors. The public contract is the same:
lookup can rely on evidence tied to the length of the specific
vector it receives.

\section{Equality Reasoning}\label{sec:dep-equality}

\begin{definition}[Equational Reasoning Combinators]\label{def:eq-reasoning}
Dependent type theory provides combinators for equational reasoning:
\begin{align*}
  \mathrm{sym} &: a = b \to b = a \\
  \mathrm{trans} &: a = b \to b = c \to a = c \\
  \mathrm{cong} &: (f : A \to B) \to a = b \to f(a) = f(b) \\
  \mathrm{subst} &: (P : A \to \mathcal{U}) \to a = b \to P(a) \to P(b)
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Proving Associativity of Addition]\label{ex:add-assoc}
In Agda, the proof that addition is associative:
\begin{verbatim}
  +-assoc : (m n p : Nat) -> (m + n) + p == m + (n + p)
  +-assoc zero n p = refl
  +-assoc (succ m) n p = cong succ (+-assoc m n p)
\end{verbatim}
The proof proceeds by induction on $m$. The base case is reflexivity (both sides reduce to $n + p$). The inductive case applies congruence to the inductive hypothesis.
\end{example}

\section{Exercises}\label{sec:dep-exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write the type of a function that takes a vector of length $n$ and returns its first element. What happens when $n = 0$?
  
  \item Define the type of sorted lists as a dependent type.
  
  \item Show that $\depFunTy{x}{\mathbb{N}}{\mathrm{Vec}(A, x) \to \mathrm{Vec}(A, x)}$ is inhabited by the identity function.
  
  \item Prove that equality is symmetric: construct a term of type $\depFunTy{a}{A}\depFunTy{b}{A}{\mathrm{Id}_A(a,b) \to \mathrm{Id}_A(b,a)}$.
  
  \item Prove that equality is transitive using the $J$ rule.
  
  \item Define a function $\mathrm{append} : \depFunTy{m}{\mathbb{N}}\depFunTy{n}{\mathbb{N}}{\mathrm{Vec}(A,m) \to \mathrm{Vec}(A,n) \to \mathrm{Vec}(A,m+n)}$.
  
  \item Show that the type $\depFunTy{n}{\mathbb{N}}{\mathrm{Id}_{\mathbb{N}}(n, n)}$ is inhabited.
  
  \item Explain why Russell's paradox prevents a universe $\mathcal{U}$ with $\mathcal{U} : \mathcal{U}$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Chapter Summary}\label{sec:dep-summary}

Dependent type theory extends the simply-typed lambda calculus with types that depend on values:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Dependent products ($\Pi$-types) generalize function types: the return type depends on the argument value.
  \item Dependent sums ($\Sigma$-types) generalize product types: the second component's type depends on the first.
  \item Identity types provide a notion of equality internal to the type theory.
  \item Inductive types allow defining data types by their constructors.
  \item Universe hierarchies avoid paradoxes.
  \item Martin-Löf's meaning explanations provide a computational interpretation.
  \item Canonicity ensures that closed terms evaluate to canonical forms.
  \item Proof assistants (Agda, Coq, Lean) are based on dependent type theory.
\end{itemize}

In the next chapter, we study the Curry--Howard correspondence in full generality, connecting proofs in logic to programs in type theory.
